VIG = "A 26-year-old truck driver from Namakkal attends a Suraksha clinic with 3 days of burning urination and a creamy urethral discharge. He had unprotected sex with a commercial sex worker 6 days ago and is married. He has no ulcers or groin swelling. He asks for 'one injection' and does not want his wife to know."

EXP = [
 explain("pathophysiology", "Why syndromic treatment works in India", "Why treat several organisms at once?",
  "Most STIs present as a small number of syndromes — urethral discharge, genital ulcer, inguinal bubo, scrotal swelling — and each syndrome has a short list of common causes. Laboratory diagnosis is often unavailable or delayed in Indian primary care, and patients may not return for results. Syndromic management treats all the likely organisms on the first visit with a colour-coded kit, which cures most patients and prevents onward spread. Urethral discharge is treated for both gonorrhoea and chlamydia because they often coexist and are hard to tell apart clinically. Genital ulcers are treated for syphilis and chancroid unless vesicles or recurrence point to herpes. Ulcers raise HIV transmission risk several-fold, which is why every visit includes an HIV test."),
 explain("signs-symptoms", "Examining the patient properly", "What must the examination include?",
  "Ask about the timing of symptoms, recent partners, condom use and previous episodes. Examine the whole genital area with good light: retract the foreskin, milk the urethra if no discharge is visible, inspect for ulcers (single or multiple, painful or painless, indurated or soft), feel the inguinal nodes, and examine the scrotum. A painful scrotal swelling of sudden onset in a young man is torsion until proved otherwise. Look in the mouth and on the skin for secondary syphilis. Partners from the previous 60 days need assessment, and the patient's wishes about disclosure must be handled with counselling rather than by skipping partner treatment."),
 explain("investigations", "Tests that change management", "Which tests matter at a Suraksha clinic?",
  "A Gram stain of a urethral smear showing intracellular Gram-negative diplococci confirms gonorrhoea on the spot. Syphilis serology (a non-treponemal test such as RPR or VDRL with a treponemal test) and an HIV test are offered to every STI client. Nucleic acid amplification tests for gonorrhoea, chlamydia, Trichomonas and Mycoplasma genitalium, with gonococcal susceptibility testing, are needed when symptoms persist after treatment, because resistance — especially in gonorrhoea — is a growing problem in India. Scrotal Doppler ultrasound helps separate epididymitis from torsion but must never delay surgery when torsion is likely."),
 explain("management", "Treat, prevent and follow up", "What does complete management look like?",
  "Give the full regimen on the day of the visit, preferably as directly observed single doses: ceftriaxone 500 mg IM with doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days for urethral discharge; benzathine penicillin 2.4 million units with azithromycin 1 g for non-herpetic ulcers (doxycycline with azithromycin if allergic to penicillin); acyclovir for herpes; and 21 days of doxycycline with azithromycin for a bubo. Promote condoms, advise abstinence until treatment is complete and partners are treated, and arrange review at 7 days. If discharge persists, ask about adherence and partners before escalating to the Trichomonas and Mycoplasma regimens and referral for testing.")]

